What is Automotive Brake Rotors?
Automotive brake rotors are circular metal discs mounted on wheel hubs that convert kinetic energy into heat through friction when brake pads clamp them, thereby slowing or stopping a vehicle. Traditionally manufactured from gray cast iron because of its durability and cost effectiveness, newer high performance models employ carbon ceramic composites and forged aluminum alloys that provide lighter weight, superior heat dissipation, and longer service intervals, albeit at higher price points.
